---
/**
 * The qualifying text for `VerifiedBadge` (src/components/VerifiedBadge.tsx),
 * rendered as real, always-visible page content — never a tooltip, never
 * `.visually-hidden` — so it is reachable on touch devices and present in the
 * accessible tree for a screen-reader user who lands directly on the badge
 * (every `VerifiedBadge` on the same page should pass this component's id as
 * its `descriptionId` prop, wiring `aria-describedby` to this paragraph).
 *
 * Every page that renders a `VerifiedBadge` MUST also render exactly one
 * `SignatureNote` (fix/verified-badge-honesty — closes the false "Verified"
 * claim called out against registry.conduitdata.io; see the PR description for
 * the before/after wording). It states plainly what the badge does and does
 * NOT mean:
 *   - what ran, at the INDEX level (S2 PR-2, real): the build runs the
 *     conduit CLI's own verifier (cmd/registry-verify, --require-root, same
 *     compiled-in trust anchors) against the index's ROOT signature, rollback
 *     floor, and 7-day staleness window, and refuses to build from anything
 *     that fails. The index every page here renders is therefore
 *     cryptographically authenticated — an index the conduit CLI would refuse
 *     cannot reach this site.
 *   - what ran, at the VERSION level: this version's entry in that verified
 *     index references a signature bundle and an SLSA provenance bundle
 *     (deriveVerified.ts).
 *   - what did NOT run: this site never downloads the connector binary and
 *     performs no cryptographic check of the artifact bytes — real
 *     verification of the actual file you install (identity-pinned Sigstore
 *     + SLSA provenance) happens at install time, in the CLI, not here.
 * Per-artifact verification at build time is planned separately (WS4 S3) —
 * this component does not claim that work is done.
 */
export const SIGNATURE_NOTE_ID = 'signature-note';
---

<p id={SIGNATURE_NOTE_ID}>
  &ldquo;Signature on file&rdquo; means this version&rsquo;s entry in the registry&rsquo;s signed
  index references a signature bundle and an SLSA provenance bundle. The index itself was
  cryptographically verified at build time: this site&rsquo;s build runs the conduit CLI&rsquo;s own
  verifier against the index&rsquo;s root signature, rollback floor, and 7-day staleness window, and
  refuses to build from anything that fails. What this site still does not do: it never downloads
  the connector binary, so it cannot confirm that the file you install today matches what was
  signed. That check happens when you run <code>conduit connectors install &lt;name&gt;</code>,
  which verifies the actual bytes against a pinned publisher identity via Sigstore and checks SLSA
  provenance before installing anything.
</p>
